# Active accounts, patient selection and cross-branch Kontrol (rule 200)

Evidence: `docs/sprints/hardening-rme-active-user-patient-selection-1.md`.
Owner decisions: `docs/decisions/rme-billing-correction-owner-decisions.md` (OD-1, OD-1a).
Extends rule 199 (visit authority from the explicit actor), rule 198 (invoice) and
rule 197 §E (payment). **Supersedes in part:** rule 197 §24, rule 198 §30a, rule 199
§14a, §16, §24, §27, §27c (see §J).

## A. Owner decisions
1. **OD-1** (owner, 2026-10-22, verbatim: *"pasien bisa kontrol lintas cabang
   bahkan dengan dokter berbeda"*): `CROSS_BRANCH_CONTROL = APPROVED`,
   `CROSS_DOCTOR_CONTROL = APPROVED`. No approval reference beyond that wording
   exists; never invent one.
2. **OD-1a** (owner answer during the sprint, 2026-10-22: *"Hanya piutang B"*): a
   patient's unpaid balance at another branch is NOT shown on, and NOT collectable
   from, a payment taken at this branch. It stays that branch's receivable,
   collected there. Collecting another branch's debt needs a NEW owner decision and
   a cash-transfer policy — never re-enable it by widening a query.

## B. Cross-branch / cross-doctor Kontrol (OD-1)
3. A Kontrol may follow a visit held at ANOTHER RME branch and treated by ANOTHER
   doctor. Never reject because the prior visit's branch or doctor differs.
4. Patient identity is global (rule 101); a Kontrol never creates a duplicate patient.
5. The prior-visit link is checked for PATIENT identity only: the prior visit must
   exist at an active RME branch (soft-deleted = absent) and belong to the SAME
   patient. `ClinicVisitRegistrationGuard::followUpViolation()` contains no branch
   or doctor comparison, and a test pins that.
6. The new visit's branch is the registrar's working branch
   (`ClinicVisitService::resolveBranchId`), never the prior visit's. Its doctor comes
   from rule §D, never from the prior visit.
7. Historical immutability: registering a Kontrol writes nothing to the prior visit,
   its medical record, consent, odontogram, invoice or payments (tests snapshot
   each row before/after).
8. Clinical history access is unchanged: the patient-centric workspace and archive
   reads keep rule 199 §15; the registration picker returns only id, number, date,
   type (+label), doctor name, initial treatment and status — never notes,
   diagnoses, payments or cash data. Front Office gains no archive access.
9. Financial ownership: the new invoice is the new visit's branch; its payments are
   that branch's. `RmeControlReceivableService` discovers carry-over (control chain
   AND patient-level receivables) ONLY at the paying visit's own branch
   (`payingBranchIds()`), fail-closed when that branch is not an active RME branch.
   `RmePaymentService` re-asserts it under the invoice lock in both allocation
   loops (a receivable of another branch is skipped). Same-branch carry-over is
   unchanged (rule 197 Phase 27.4 / Sprint 62.2 FIFO).

## C. Patient selection at the authoritative boundary
10. `ClinicVisitRegistrationGuard` is the ONE source of the registration selection
    rules. `StoreClinicVisitRequest` turns its verdict into a form error;
    `ClinicVisitService::create()` re-asserts it against the explicit registrar.
    Never restate the prior-visit lookup in a caller (a test pins it).
10a. A new-patient registration must really create the patient: `create()` drops
    any `patient_id` in new mode and refuses a `new_patient` payload that is not an
    array, so it can never fall through to an existing patient unchecked.
11. Existing patient: `assertPatientSelectable()` = `PatientSelectorSearchService::
    isSelectable($registrar, $id)` — global RME identity, doctor patient scope,
    merged and soft-deleted excluded, fail-closed for a context-bound registrar with
    no working branch. A nonexistent, deleted, out-of-registry or non-positive id is
    refused (`patient_id`).
12. Order inside the transaction: merge guard (FOR SHARE, its own message) →
    selectability → patient resolution → prior-visit check with the prior visit
    read FOR SHARE → insert. A refusal writes nothing.
13. The prior visit is read FOR SHARE so a concurrent soft-delete / re-point (FOR NO
    KEY UPDATE) waits. Verify with a FOR NO KEY UPDATE probe, never FOR UPDATE —
    the insert's foreign key takes FOR KEY SHARE, which a FOR UPDATE probe would hit
    even with no lock taken (vacuous pass).
14. Incoherent input is refused: a follow-up type without a prior visit; a prior
    visit under a non-follow-up type; a prior visit for a brand-new patient (checked
    before the patient row is created).

## D. Doctor and room at registration
15. A front-desk registration (Admin Klinik / Perawat / Front Office working context)
    carries NO doctor — any supplied doctor, the previous one included, is dropped;
    the doctor is resolved from the room on `assignRoom()` (Sprint 66.1.4).
16. Every other registrar must name a doctor online at the visit branch
    (`assertDoctorSelectableForVisit`), at the service as well as the form, because a
    visit's doctor widens that doctor's patient scope (rule 199 F9).
17. Registration NEVER writes a room: `clinic_room_id` is not a
    `StoreClinicVisitRequest` field and `create()` drops it. A crafted POST used to
    place a new visit into another branch's room with an existence check only.
18. The visit edit is an ALLOW-list (`ClinicVisitService::EDITABLE_FIELDS` =
    chief_complaint, initial_treatment_id, initial_service_note). Patient, prior
    visit, branch, type, numbering, creator, room, doctor and status are fixed at
    registration or moved only by their own paths. Never return to a strip-list.

## E. Active accounts
19. Account status = `users.is_active` + SoftDeletes `deleted_at`. There is no
    "suspended" state; never invent one.
20. `App\Support\AccessControl\ActiveAccountResolver` is the ONE definition of a
    usable account: persisted row, `is_active`, not soft-deleted, re-read from the
    database. `ClinicVisitActorGuard` and `RmeBillingActorGuard` delegate to it;
    nothing else may restate `where('is_active', true)` for an actor (a test pins the
    three consumers).
21. Login form: `LoginRequest` uses `Auth::attemptWhen`; the "Akun Anda tidak aktif"
    message is shown only after the password is proven. Wrong password, unknown
    address and soft-deleted account keep the generic `auth.failed`. A refused
    inactive attempt counts against the rate limiter.
22. Live session: `EnsureAccountIsActive` (appended to `web`, second after
    `EnsureDoctorSessionLease`, before `TouchOnlineContextLastSeen` and
    `EnsureRmeOnlineContext`) re-reads the account on every authenticated request.
    A deactivated account is stopped BEFORE any controller: presence dropped (room
    freed), its doctor lease released (`account_inactive`), one PII-free audit row
    (`INACTIVE_ACCOUNT_SESSION_TERMINATED`, entity `users`), logout (cycles the
    remember token → every remember-me cookie of the account dies), session
    invalidated, CSRF token rotated; redirect to login (JSON: 401). Bookkeeping
    failures are reported, never turned into a 500, never leave the session alive.
23. NEVER name a middleware method `terminate()` unless it is a terminable hook: the
    HTTP kernel calls `terminate($request, $response)` on every middleware after
    every response, whatever its visibility (found here: a private `terminate()`
    turned every request into a 500).
24. Remember-me: a cookie of a deactivated account is torn down by §22 on the
    restoring request; a soft-deleted account is never restored (provider scope).
25. Android app login (`DoctorAppLoginService`) refuses a deactivated ACCOUNT even
    when its doctor record is active (`account_inactive`, after the password, before
    anything is written); ticket redemption re-checks it. Both browser WebAuthn
    completions (`DoctorDeviceWebAuthnLoginService` and
    `FrontOfficeDeviceWebAuthnLoginService::completeLogin`) re-check it before
    anything is written, because an account can be deactivated between the password
    step and the fingerprint. Any NEW login path must do the same.
26. Permission or role revoked from an active account takes effect on the next
    request: the session holds only the id and the user, roles and permissions are
    re-read per request (Spatie flushes its cache through its own API; a raw SQL
    edit of `role_has_permissions` needs `permission:cache-reset`).
27. Branch authority: a context-bound account whose working branch is deactivated,
    not RME-enabled, or whose context ended gets an EMPTY scope (fail closed). The
    doctor branch lock, temporary cover and FO pin are unchanged.
28. Not affected: guest pages, `/login`, health endpoints, the stateless device API
    group, webhooks outside `web`. Doctor trusted-device, WebAuthn, branch-lock and
    single-session flags are NOT changed by this rule.

## F. Preserved (regression-verified)
29. Invoice and payment explicit actor (rules 197/198), manual pricing D21 (rule
    194), D10 payment block, invoice-version binding, immutable snapshots, financial
    audit, receivable totals.
30. SPN4 correction pilot: maker user 29, checker user 1, SPN4 only (rules 195–197).
31. Doctor device/branch restrictions (rules 138, 143, 154, 160) and RME clinical
    integrity (consent gate, room gate, SOAP hidden).
32. Feature flags: none added, none changed.

## G. Data confidentiality and audit
33. Audit payloads carry ids and reason codes only — never a name, e-mail, NIK,
    clinical text or session id. A terminated session writes exactly one row; the
    next request is a guest and writes nothing.
34. The patient picker and the payment screen never reveal another branch's
    invoices, payments or cash data.

## H. Concurrency
35. PostgreSQL proves the prior-visit FOR SHARE lock
    (`ClinicVisitRegistrationConcurrencyTest`, skips off PostgreSQL — never passes).
    Payment loops keep their invoice row locks; lock order unchanged.

## I. Verification, release, rollback
36. CI: tokens `ActiveAccountSession|ClinicVisitPatientSelection|CrossBranchKontrol|
    ClinicVisitRegistrationConcurrency|PatientOutstandingReceivableCarryOver|
    RmeControlVisitReceivableCarryOverPayment` in BOTH critical-gate variants; the six
    suites declared in `config/ci_runner.php` `critical_gate_mandatory_suites`.
37. Deploy only via `scripts/deploy-vps-runner.sh` executed INSIDE the VPS; complete
    only at `exit=0` + `DEPLOY OK` + `DEPLOY_HEAD_TARGET_MATCH=YES`. No migration.
38. Production verification is read-only (code fingerprint, routes, flags, pilot
    config, clinical + financial fingerprint). Never deactivate a real account,
    create a patient/visit, or impersonate a doctor to produce evidence.
39. Rollback: `scripts/rollback-vps.sh audit-rme-clinic-visit-branch-authority-1-go`;
    no data to restore. Never "fix" a post-deploy access problem by removing an
    is_active check or widening a branch query.
